  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 233 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA upper level trough will move across the nrn Rockies today thru \ntonight with SW flow aloft across the area. Monsoon moisture will\nbe embedded in the flow with PW values 150 to 175 percent above \nnormal. MLCAPE will be around 1000 j/kg from the Palmer Divide \nacross the far ern plains this aftn with lower values further \nwest. Overall, will see a good chance of showers/tstms this aftn \nthru the evening hours, especially across the plains. There is \nenough shear for an isold svr storm or two where the highest \nMLCAPE will exist this aftn. In addition, with decent PW's some \nlocally heavy rainfall will be possible as well. Highs this aftn \nwill be in the upper 80's to mid 90's across the plains.\n\nOn Tue, the flow aloft will become more westerly as a wek cool front \nmoves across the plains.  There will still be some monsoon moisture \nembedded in the flow, however, PW values will decrease, especially \nacross the nrn half of the area.  Best chc for showers/tstms will be \nover the Palmer Divide and sern areas of the plains.  MLCAPE is fcst \nto be in the 500-800 j/kg range, however, shear will be better so \ncan't rule out an isold svr storm over the Palmer Divide.  Highs \nacross the plains will be in the 85 to 90 degree range.\n\nFor Wed, the flow aloft will become more WNW as a surge of cooler \nair moves across late Tue night into Wed morning.  This will lead \nmainly dry conditions with highs across nern CO in the upper 70's to \nmid 80's.\n\nLooking ahead to Thu and Fri, the flow aloft will be mainly zonal \nwith minimal moisture embedded in the flow.  As a result, expect \nmainly dry conditions both days with warmer temperatures as readings \nover nern CO rise back into the upper 80's to lower 90s.\n\nFor next weekend, the flow aloft will remain zonal on Sat as another \nweak front moves across.  As a result, highs will drop back into the \n80's across the plains.  Meanwhile, will leave in a slight chc for \naftn tstms across the area.  On Sun, the flow aloft will become more \nSW as an upper level trough moves into the nrn Rockies.  The GFS is \nfaster with moving a cold front across the area during the day while \nthe ECMWF is slower.  With SW flow aloft and upper level trough \nstill to the NW of the area, ECMWF probably has a better handle on \ntiming of front.  Thus may see highs reach the upper 80's to lower \n90's over nern CO on Sun before it reaches the area Sun night.",
